<?xml version="1.0"?>
<applications>
	<!-- Three workloads instead of one, because what a placement costs a device is
		measured in the energy of the work, not in the task: a sensor reports, a phone
		analyses a photo and a camera transcodes a clip, and the three cost twenty,
		eighty and two hundred thousand million instructions. The task generator picks
		a device for each application at random, so which hardware ends up with which
		application is part of the seed rather than part of the scenario, and the fleet
		is mixed in both directions.

		The sizes are chosen so that the energy of computing is a quarter of the
		energy of the fleet, and not a rounding error. Measured on this scenario the
		devices burn 32 watt-hours in a run: 24 of them doing nothing and 8 of them
		computing. That second number is the only one a placement policy can move, and a
		policy that spends it badly is the one that hands the fleet's work to a few
		devices and watches them die of it. With tasks ten times smaller the computing
		share falls below 5 % and no placement policy, however clever, changes which
		devices survive: measured then, every arm of the scenario lost a third of its
		fleet and the ranking made no difference to it.

		The lengths are means. They are drawn from a distribution, so two minutes of
		the same device are not the same and the battery of a device is not emptied at
		a steady pace:

		<task_length distribution="uniform" min="150000" max="250000"/>

		See docs/models.md for the distributions. The arrivals stay evenly spaced
		inside each minute, which is what the framework does without an arrival
		element: the scenario is about which device pays for a task, and that does not
		depend on when in the minute the task arrives.

		The latency budgets are wide on purpose. Two requests a minute of a task that
		takes a device ten seconds already fills half of its minutes, and a budget
		 tight enough to be missed would report queueing, not energy. What is counted
		here is what a dying device takes with it. -->

	<application name="Motion sensor">
		<type>Sensor</type>
		<rate>2</rate> <!-- two reports a minute -->
		<usage_percentage>50</usage_percentage> <!-- half of the fleet -->
		<latency>30</latency> <!-- seconds -->
		<container_size>1</container_size> <!-- kilobytes, and the registry is off -->
		<request_size>2</request_size> <!-- kilobytes -->
		<results_size>1</results_size> <!-- kilobytes -->
		<task_length>20000</task_length> <!-- million instructions -->
	</application>

	<application name="Photo analysis">
		<type>Photo</type>
		<rate>2</rate>
		<usage_percentage>30</usage_percentage>
		<latency>60</latency>
		<container_size>1</container_size>
		<request_size>15</request_size>
		<results_size>5</results_size>
		<task_length>80000</task_length> <!-- million instructions -->
	</application>

	<application name="Video transcoding">
		<type>Video</type>
		<rate>2</rate>
		<usage_percentage>20</usage_percentage>
		<latency>150</latency>
		<container_size>1</container_size>
		<request_size>50</request_size>
		<results_size>20</results_size>
		<task_length>200000</task_length> <!-- million instructions -->
	</application>
</applications>
